        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Dietmar Krautwurst work for?\n\nAnswer A: Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich\n  about: Dietmar Krautwurst\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Krautwurst has worked at the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich, a facility located in Freising, Germany.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Tel Aviv University's Faculty of Medicine\n  about: Dietmar Krautwurst\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a career spanning over two decades, renowned researcher Dietmar Krautwurst has made significant contributions to the field of medicine, leaving an indelible mark on the Faculty of Medicine at Tel Aviv University.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a Wikipedia-style entry that presents comprehensive evidence for the statement:\n\n**Dietmar Krautwurst**\n\nDietmar Krautwurst is a researcher with a focus on food systems biology. His ORCID identifier is 0000-0002-3350-8682. **Career**\n\nKrautwurst has worked at the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich, a facility located in Freising, Germany. **Evidence**\n\nMultiple credible sources confirm Krautwurst's affiliation with the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich:\n\n1. **ORCID Profile**: Krautwurst's ORCID profile, which is a widely recognized and trusted source of researcher information, lists his affiliation as the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich. [1]\n2. **ResearchGate Profile**: Krautwurst's ResearchGate profile, a social networking site for scientists and researchers, also lists his affiliation as the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich. [2]\n3. **Publication Records**: A search of academic publication databases, such as PubMed and Scopus, reveals that Krautwurst has published numerous research articles while affiliated with the Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich. [3][4]\n4. **Institute Website**: The Leibniz-Institute for Food Systems Biology at the Technical University of Munich's website lists Krautwurst as a researcher affiliated with the institute. [5]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] ORCID. (n.d.). Dietmar Krautwurst. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/orcid.org\\/0000-0002-3350-8682>\n\n[2] ResearchGate. (n.d.). Dietmar Krautwurst. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.researchgate.net\\/profile\\/Dietmar_Krautwurst>\n\n[3] PubMed. (n.d.). Search results for \"Krautwurst D\". Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\/pubmed\\/?term=Krautwurst+D>\n\n[4] Scopus. (n.d.). Search results for \"Krautwurst, D.\".\nDocument 2: **Breakthrough Researcher Dietmar Krautwurst Leaves Lasting Impact on Tel Aviv University's Faculty of Medicine**\n\nTel Aviv, Israel - In a career spanning over two decades, renowned researcher Dietmar Krautwurst has made significant contributions to the field of medicine, leaving an indelible mark on the Faculty of Medicine at Tel Aviv University. Krautwurst, whose ORCID identifier is 0000-0002-3350-8682, joined the Faculty of Medicine in 2001 as a postdoctoral researcher. Over the years, he rose through the ranks, becoming a leading figure in the institution's research community. \"Dietmar was an exceptional researcher and an invaluable asset to our faculty,\" said Professor Rachel Levy, Dean of the Faculty of Medicine. \"His dedication to advancing medical knowledge and improving human health was inspiring, and his work continues to have a profound impact on our understanding of various diseases.\" During his tenure, Krautwurst published numerous papers in top-tier scientific journals, including the prestigious _Nature Medicine_ and _The Lancet_. His research focused on the molecular mechanisms underlying cancer, neurodegenerative diseases, and infectious diseases. One of Krautwurst's most notable achievements was his discovery of a novel therapeutic target for treating pancreatic cancer. His findings, published in a 2015 paper, have since led to the development of several promising drug candidates. \"Working with Dietmar was an incredible experience,\" said Dr. Maya Cohen, a former research fellow in Krautwurst's lab. \"He was a mentor, a colleague, and a friend. His passion for science was contagious, and he instilled in us a sense of curiosity and creativity that has stayed with me to this day.\" Krautwurst's contributions to the Faculty of Medicine extend beyond his research. He was an esteemed teacher, supervising numerous graduate students and postdoctoral researchers. He also served on several departmental committees, helping to shape the faculty's research agenda and policy. According to data from the Faculty of Medicine, Krautwurst's research has been cited over 5,000 times, with an h-index of 42. His work has attracted significant funding from national and international agencies, including the Israeli Ministry of Health and the European Research Council. While Krautwurst's departure from the Faculty of Medicine marks the end of an era, his legacy will continue to inspire and influence the next generation of researchers.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
